-> Machine language / Assembly language / High level language

ML: The lowest-level language written in binary (0s and 1s), directly understood by the computer hardware (CPU)
AL: Slightly higher level than machine language, Uses mnemonics (short codes) instead of binary
HLL: Closest to human language, Easy to read, write, and understand

Machine Language → Talking in pure numbers (0/1)
Assembly Language → Using short commands (codes)
High-Level Language → Speaking English-like language

✅ Final Summary
Machine → fastest but hardest
Assembly → easier but still low-level
High-level → easiest and most widely used
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-> Left-Shift Operator

All binary digits shifting to Left Side: <- Left

6 << 1 = 12 : x = 1
6 << 2 = 24 : x = 2
6 << 3 = 48 : Formula (6 * (2^x))

-> Right-Shift Operator

All binary digits shifting to Right Side: -> Right

6 >> 2 : x=2
Formula : (6 / (2^x))
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-> Decimal to Binary
int main(){
    int n;
    cin>>n;
    int ans = 0;
    int mul = 1;
    while(n>0){
        int rem = n%2;
        n = n/2;
        ans = rem*mul + ans;
        mul = mul*10;  //this will keep increasing power of 10
    }
    cout<< ans; 
    return 0;
}

-> Binary to Decimal

int main(){
    int n;
    cin>>n;
    int ans = 0;
    int mul = 1;
    //while(num) still its vaild, as it coinsider true only, > 0(false)
    while(n > 0){   
        int rem = n%10;
        n = n/10;
        ans = rem * mul + ans;
        mul = mul * 2;
    }
    cout<<ans;
    return 0;
}
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-> // Prime
int main() {
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    if(n <= 1){
        cout << "Not Prime";
        return 0;
    }
    for(int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++){
        if(n % i == 0){
            cout << "Not Prime";
            return 0;
        }
    }
    cout << "Prime";
    return 0;
}

-> Factorial
int main(){
    int n;
    cin>>n;
    int ans = 1;
    for(int i = 1; i<=n; i++){
        ans = ans *i;
    }
    cout<< ans;
    
    return 0;
}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------->

-> Pass by Value vs Refrence

In pass by value, a copy of the variable is passed, so changes do not affect the original variable. 
In pass by reference, the actual variable is passed using references (&), so any changes inside the function modify the original variable.

Feature	                |  Pass by Value      |  Pass by Reference
What is passed          |  Copy	              |  Original variable
Memory	                |  New memory created |	 Same memory
Changes affect original?|	❌ No	         | ✅ Yes
Syntax	                |  int x	          |  int &x

// Pass By value
void update(int x){
    x = 10;
}
int main(){
    int a = 5;
    update(a);
    cout << a;   // Output: 5
    return 0;
}

// Pass By Refrence
void update(int &x){
    x = 10;
}
int main(){
    int a = 5;
    update(a);
    cout << a;   // Output: 10
    return 0;
}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-> Memory Consumption in Array

For Integer: [2, 7, 3, 8]
Imagine, first address is 500, therfore as int consume 4 bytes, so [500, 504, 508, 516]

For Char: ['d', 'y', 'k', 'r']
Imagine, first address is 500, therfore as cahr consume 1 bytes, so [500, 501, 502, 503]
